<h1>Signal Path: IQ → Magnitude</h1>
<p class="tagline">How raw USB bytes from the dongle become the uint16_t magnitude values that the decoder actually reads — and how to debug when that pipeline breaks.</p>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="1">
    <span>1. What Are IQ Samples?</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>Radio signals are waves. To capture them digitally a receiver doesn't just sample the amplitude — it captures two components simultaneously: the <strong>In-phase (I)</strong> and <strong>Quadrature (Q)</strong> channels, offset by 90 degrees. Together they describe the full vector of the signal at every moment.</p>

<p>The RTL-SDR dongle produces these as pairs of unsigned 8-bit values (called <strong>UC8</strong> — Unsigned Complex 8-bit). Each byte represents one channel, biased around 127.5 (so 0 = −1.0, 127 ≈ 0, 255 = +1.0). The USB transfer gives readsb a raw byte stream: <code>I, Q, I, Q, I, Q, …</code></p>

<p>For Mode S decoding, readsb doesn't care about phase — it only cares about <strong>signal magnitude</strong> (signal strength at each moment). Magnitude is calculated as:</p>

<pre><span class="cm">// mathematically: mag = sqrt(I² + Q²), normalized to 0..1</span>
fI = (raw_I - 127.5) / 127.5;  <span class="cm">// normalize to -1..+1</span>
fQ = (raw_Q - 127.5) / 127.5;
magsq = fI*fI + fQ*fQ;
mag   = sqrtf(magsq);           <span class="cm">// 0..1 range</span>

<span class="cm">// stored as uint16_t scaled to 0..65535</span>
output = (uint16_t)(mag * 65535.0f + 0.5f);</pre>

<div class="callout">
  <strong>Why uint16_t?</strong> A 16-bit integer gives enough resolution to distinguish signal from noise, avoids floating-point math in the hot demodulator loop, and keeps the buffer compact — 12 buffers × 128 KiB each still fits comfortably in L3 cache.
</div>

<!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<h2>
  <label class="section-header">
    <input type="checkbox" class="section-check" data-section="2">
    <span>2. The Lookup Table Trick — convert_uc8_nodc()</span>
    <span class="section-label">(read)</span>
  </label>
</h2>

<p>Computing <code>sqrt()</code> for every sample pair at 2.4 million samples per second would be expensive. readsb pre-computes all 256×256 = 65536 possible (I, Q) combinations at startup and stores the result in a table: <span class="filepath">convert.c:35</span></p>

<pre><span class="kw">static</span> <span class="ty">uint16_t</span> *uc8_lookup;   <span class="cm">// 65536 entries, each precomputed</span>

<span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">int</span> i = <span class="num">0</span>; i &lt;= <span class="num">255</span>; i++) {
    <span class="kw">for</span> (<span class="kw">int</span> q = <span class="num">0</span>; q &lt;= <span class="num">255</span>; q++) {
        fI = (i - <span class="num">127.5</span>) / <span class="num">127.5</span>;
        fQ = (q - <span class="num">127.5</span>) / <span class="num">127.5</span>;
        magsq = fI * fI + fQ * fQ;
        <span class="kw">if</span> (magsq &gt; <span class="num">1</span>) magsq = <span class="num">1</span>;   <span class="cm">// clamp to valid range</span>
        <span class="cm">// store as uint16_t</span>
        uc8_lookup[(i * <span class="num">256</span>) + q] = (<span class="ty">uint16_t</span>)(sqrtf(magsq) * <span class="num">65535.0f</span> + <span class="num">0.5f</span>);
    }
}</pre>

<p>At runtime, each sample pair is just a single table lookup: <span class="filepath">convert.c:81</span></p>

<pre>mag = uc8_lookup[*in++];   <span class="cm">// *in is uint16_t* pointing to interleaved I,Q pairs</span>
*mag_data++ = mag;</pre>

